<div class="mw-parser-output"><h1>Pole Arms</h1>
<p>The pole arm was developed in order to put infantry on even ground with the cavalry. In other words, to give the ground troops the same advantage as their mounted opponents. Pole arms were typically held two-handed at the rear part of the shaft higher than the front, so the points which glanced off armor would not go uselessly into the air but would be forced downward into rider or mount - at worst, into the ground to form a barrier.
</p><p>Other pole arms gave way to pike and halberd for one or two reasons. Those with massive heads were not as efficient as the pike. When their shafts were lengthened past a certain limit, they were too cumbersome to wield. (Spear-type pole arms were lengthened to pikes and were then called just that - there are ox-tongued and spetum-like heads, but the pike shaft is too long for useful employment of ranseur or partisan heads.) Those which were shortened for use as cleaving weapons were not as efficient as the halberd, or were changed so that they became almost indistinguishable from the halberd (typically guisarme-voulge forms).
</p><p>The evolution of the pole arm reflects the trends in armor and tactics in medieval warfare. It is also of great help in understanding why battles were fought as they were and can help to explain some of the outcomes.
</p>
<h2><span class="mw-headline" id="In_Valhalla">In Valhalla</span></h2>
<p>In Valhalla the polearms are one of the six families of weapons (see <a cmd='#'>Help Weapons</a>), and each has a skill of its own: the quarterstaff, spear, javelin, lance, trident, halberd, bardiche and scythe. They pierce, slash or bludgeon according to their heads, and most are two-handed. Other pole arms, such as the pike, glaive or partisan, have no skill of their own: look at one to see which skill it needs.
</p><p><br />
